1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,784
Thursday, ten to eleven at night.

2
00:00:02,834 --> 00:00:09,720
A buyer pays Malika for a wedding bouquet, and ecom asks the card zone to charge the card.

3
00:00:09,770 --> 00:00:11,592
Then nothing.

4
00:00:11,642 --> 00:00:19,824
The card processing centre is slow, or its reply is lost on the way, and the bank's chosen waiting time runs out.

5
00:00:19,874 --> 00:00:22,560
The buyer's card may already be charged.

6
00:00:22,610 --> 00:00:24,432
Or it may not.

7
00:00:24,482 --> 00:00:37,680
In Lesson 27, a charge like this was marked unknown, never declined, and in Lesson 8, every charge carried our own payment id, so a centre can be asked about it later.

8
00:00:37,730 --> 00:00:44,472
This lesson asks that question, patiently, until the charge is found or a person decides.

9
00:00:44,522 --> 00:00:47,040
There are three tempting mistakes here.

10
00:00:47,090 --> 00:00:52,584
Call it declined, and the buyer pays again, so the card is charged twice.

11
00:00:52,634 --> 00:00:56,568
Send the charge again, and the result is the same.

12
00:00:56,618 --> 00:01:02,472
Wait in the app's memory, and the app restarts, and nobody ever finds out.

13
00:01:02,522 --> 00:01:08,736
I think silence is the hardest case in payments, because every quick answer to it is wrong.

14
00:01:08,786 --> 00:01:15,104
When the network goes silent, what should the bank do with the charge?

15
00:01:15,154 --> 00:01:17,840
So the first rule is a refusal.

16
00:01:17,890 --> 00:01:25,160
An unknown charge is never declined, and never sent again, because the money may already have moved.

17
00:01:25,210 --> 00:01:28,520
The buyer's page tells them not to pay again.

18
00:01:28,570 --> 00:01:31,520
Then a durable workflow takes over.

19
00:01:31,570 --> 00:01:36,320
It is a task that remembers where it is, even when the app restarts.

20
00:01:36,370 --> 00:01:42,608
There is exactly one for each payment, so two copies can never chase the same charge.

21
00:01:42,658 --> 00:01:48,872
It asks the card processing centre about this one charge only, by the payment's own id.

22
00:01:48,922 --> 00:01:50,576
These are questions only.

23
00:01:50,626 --> 00:01:52,448
They never move money.

24
00:01:52,498 --> 00:01:58,136
Between questions it waits, and each wait is longer than the one before.

25
00:01:58,186 --> 00:02:00,152
That pattern is backoff.

26
00:02:00,202 --> 00:02:03,536
It gives a struggling network room to recover.

27
00:02:03,586 --> 00:02:05,408
Found means paid.

28
00:02:05,458 --> 00:02:10,184
Not found means declined, but only once the network has had time to know.

29
00:02:10,234 --> 00:02:16,112
If the charge is found after the buyer's page has closed, the order is already over.

30
00:02:16,162 --> 00:02:18,464
So the money goes back to the card.

31
00:02:18,514 --> 00:02:24,968
The questions stop before the card processing centre's daily cutoff, less the bank's chosen margin.

32
00:02:25,018 --> 00:02:28,064
That leaves time to give money back the same day.

33
00:02:28,114 --> 00:02:31,760
Anything still unknown then goes to manual review.

34
00:02:31,810 --> 00:02:36,152
A person at the bank checks with the network, and records the answer.

35
00:02:36,202 --> 00:02:40,328
Here is the wedding bouquet, as everyone sees it.

36
00:02:40,378 --> 00:02:46,856
On the buyer's phone, the page says we're confirming your payment, and adds: don't pay again.

37
00:02:46,906 --> 00:02:51,752
In Malika's merchant portal, the status reads checking with the bank.

38
00:02:51,802 --> 00:02:57,680
She opens it, and the calls to the card scheme show one debit line, with no answer.

39
00:02:57,730 --> 00:03:01,544
Most nights, the workflow gets its answer a little later.

40
00:03:01,594 --> 00:03:07,112
The status changes to paid, or to declined, and Malika is told.

41
00:03:07,162 --> 00:03:09,512
Tonight is the rare case.

42
00:03:09,562 --> 00:03:13,400
The day's deadline nears, and there is still no answer.

43
00:03:13,450 --> 00:03:16,640
The status changes to being checked by a person.

44
00:03:16,690 --> 00:03:23,336
In the bank's admin portal, Dilnoza sees the payment under needs review, with how long it has waited.

45
00:03:23,386 --> 00:03:27,488
She asks the network, then picks debited or not debited.

46
00:03:27,538 --> 00:03:31,832
For debited, she enters the network's reference for the charge.

47
00:03:31,882 --> 00:03:37,472
Either way, she writes a note: who at the network confirmed it, and how.

48
00:03:37,522 --> 00:03:39,992
That note is saved for audit.

49
00:03:40,042 --> 00:03:44,432
She presses resolve, and the payment takes its final state.

50
00:03:44,482 --> 00:03:48,536
By morning, the buyer has paid once, or not at all.

51
00:03:48,586 --> 00:03:54,728
Malika ships a bouquet only if it was paid, and never refuses one that was.

52
00:03:54,778 --> 00:03:58,256
The bank can explain every charge at the end of the day.

53
00:03:58,306 --> 00:04:02,600
And none of it depended on the app staying up, or on anyone remembering.

54
00:04:02,650 --> 00:04:08,648
To recap: an unknown charge is asked about, never declined and never sent again.

55
00:04:08,698 --> 00:04:14,704
And if the network never answers, who decides in the end?

56
00:04:14,754 --> 00:04:18,016
A person at the bank, who records the answer.

57
00:04:18,066 --> 00:04:20,968
Next: how the shop hears about a payment.
